            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Beginnings and Personal Approach</strong><br>\nDiane Arbus was born in 1923 in New York City into a wealthy family. She began her career in photography with her husband, Allan Arbus, producing fashion images. However, she soon branched out on her own, developing a style marked by a direct, stark portrayal of her subjects.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Focus on Marginalized Individuals</strong><br>\nArbus is best known for her powerful, often unsettling images of people on the fringes of society, such as circus performers, transgender people, and individuals with mental illnesses. She sought to reveal the humanity of her subjects, portraying them with dignity and depth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Artistic Recognition and Method</strong><br>\nArbus's work gained recognition in the 1960s as she worked on assignment for magazines and pursued her personal projects. She was known for her square-format photographs taken with a Rolleiflex camera, which allowed for a close, intimate connection with her subjects.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Major Exhibitions and Publications</strong><br>\nHer work was exhibited at major institutions, including the Museum of Modern Art in New York. Arbus was also featured in \"Esquire,\" \"Harper’s Bazaar,\" and other magazines, contributing to her reputation as a fearless and original artist.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Teaching and Influence</strong><br>\nArbus taught photography in the 1960s and influenced a generation of photographers with her bold, unflinching style and her approach to the medium as a means of exploring existential questions through portraiture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Themes of Identity and Normalcy</strong><br>\nArbus's photographs often challenge the viewer's notions of normalcy and beauty, probing the boundaries of what society considers acceptable. Her work is a powerful commentary on social norms and the human condition.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
