          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Controversy and Critique</strong><br>\nWhile Arbus's work has been both praised for its bravery and criticized for its perceived exploitation, she maintained that her goal was to document and understand, rather than exploit her subjects.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Fame</strong><br>\nArbus committed suicide in 1971, but her influence only grew after her death. She became the first American photographer to be represented at the Venice Biennale in 1972, and her work has continued to be the subject of extensive critical discussion and public fascination.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enduring Legacy</strong><br>\nDiane Arbus's legacy as a photographer lies in her ability to transcend the mere documentation of her subjects to reveal the complexities and innate dignity of human life, making her one of the most important photographers of her generation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
